# Reproducible file bytes across Windows, Linux and CI.
#
# ROOT CAUSE of the previous Windows/Linux hash divergence
# -------------------------------------------------------
# There was no .gitattributes and `core.autocrlf=true`, so git stored LF in the index
# but checked CRLF into the working tree. `metadata.source_sha256` is computed from the
# WORKING-TREE bytes (Path.read_bytes() in src/experiment_support.py), so the artifacts
# recorded hashes of CRLF content. On a Linux clone (autocrlf=false/input) the working
# tree is LF, the recomputed hash differs, and `validate_metadata` fails — meaning
# check_lab.py passed only on the machine that produced the artifacts.
#
# FIX
# ---
# Force LF in the working tree on every platform, so the working tree always equals the
# blob and Path.read_bytes() is deterministic. `.gitattributes` is committed BEFORE the
# renormalization commit, so a fresh clone of any commit from here on gets LF.
#
# Verified after the change with: git ls-files --eol   (every tracked text file w/lf)
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rules: a change to `* text=auto` detection can then never silently flip the
# line endings of the file types this project hashes, diffs or validates.
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
*.co            text eol=lf
*.example       text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# Binary payloads must never be converted: the corpus PDFs and any model/weight blob.
*.pdf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.onnx  binary
*.gguf  binary
*.zip   binary
